summary:
Castella, Grossi, Lee and Skinner prove the anticyclotomic Iwasawa main conjecture for a rational elliptic curve E at an Eisenstein prime p, that is a good prime at which E has a rational p-isogeny with E[p]^ss = F_p(φ) ⊕ F_p(ψ). Theorem C gives the BDP form: char_Λ(𝔛_E)Λ^ur = (L_E) for K with (Heeg), (spl), (disc), (Sel) and φ|_{G_p} ≠ 1, ω. Corollary D gives Perrin-Riou's Heegner-point form. The method follows Greenberg–Vatsal. Residual comparisons show that 𝔛_E and L_E both have μ = 0 and the same λ as the corresponding objects for φ and ψ: §1 via Rubin's main conjecture for K and Hida's μ = 0, §2 via Kriz's congruence L_E ≡ (𝓔^ι)²L_φ². So one divisibility suffices, and §§3–4 prove it with a Howard-style Kolyvagin system argument for twists by anticyclotomic characters, which bounds lengths without residual irreducibility. Applications: a p-converse to Gross–Zagier–Kolyvagin at Eisenstein primes in ranks 0 and 1 (Theorem E), rank-0 and rank-1 quadratic twists of curves with a rational 3-isogeny (Corollary 5.2.3), and the p-part of BSD in analytic rank one (Theorem F). The atlas plans much of this:
- RankZeroOneBSD BSD.7/BSD.7a own the Eisenstein-prime main conjectures and BSD formulas;
- HeegnerPointEulerSystems HE.8/HE.8b own Heegner classes and the anticyclotomic main conjectures;
- GrossZagierAndArithmeticHeights GZ.8/GZ.9 own the complex and p-adic Gross–Zagier formulas;
- EulerSystemsAndKolyvaginSystems ES.4/ES.8 own Kolyvagin-system bounds.
So most routes are sources. Rubin's imaginary-quadratic main conjecture joins the pending CM main-conjecture Part II, and the p-converse joins Skinner's RankOneConverse Part II. Issues found:
- the p = 7 example curve (conductor 574) has bad reduction at 7;
- the Goldfeld-type corollary is not proved for curves with v_3(j) < 0;
- L_ψ, whose conductor is divisible by p, is used but never defined.
